Could reflection get credentialMap and put source into target authenticator. This simulates encrypted sync. But test-only reflection okay.

However requirement "3 种设备下注册后另一设备可无密码登录" maybe simpler parameterized device types and each registers same user via `/user/credential/register` after first signup. Then fourth target can choose any synced credential. But cross-device sync layer should create sync envelopes.

What production code should actually do? We can create a credible feature package `io.supertokens.passkeysync`:
- `DeviceType` enum: `DESKTOP`, `MOBILE`, `TABLET` (3 kinds) maybe platform labels.
- `TrustedDevice` data class: deviceId, userId, deviceType, platform, publicKey? `parentDeviceId`, `trustDepth`, `trusted`, `revoked`, `registeredAt`, `trustedAt`, `previousHash`, `chainHash`.
- `TrustChainLink` / `DeviceTrustChain`: append-only SHA-256 chain. Verify sequence, previous hash, genesis, signatures maybe HMAC? We can use SHA-256 and optional Ed25519 signatures. To keep tests no key management complexity, implement hash chain with server-stored records. But "device trust chain" ideally each link signed by device. We can support Ed25519 device key pairs and verify signatures. JDK 21 has `KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("Ed25519")`, `Signature.getInstance("Ed25519")`. Device enrollment:
  - First device registers with device public key, self-signed genesis attestation.
  - Additional devices need an existing trusted device to sign an endorsement over new device id/public key. Server verifies endorsement against parent public key and appends link. This forms trust chain.
  - Revocation also signed by trusted device? Could support.
- `PasskeySyncEnvelope`: syncId, userId, sourceDeviceId, targetDeviceId, credentialId, encryptedPayload? `wrappedCredential`, `payloadHash`, `createdAt`, consumed? We should not invent actual encryption. We can accept `encryptedCredentialBlob` and `wrappingKeyId`; server stores only ciphertext/hash, never plaintext. For tests, blob can be base64 of copied credential source or arbitrary token. Cross-device sync "commit" and "receive/ack".
- Service persists via a `PasskeySyncRepository` interface with default `UserMetadataPasskeySyncRepository` storing JSON in user metadata key `passkey_sync_chain_v1` and `passkey_sync_envelopes_v1`. But user metadata values likely JsonObject; we can store arrays? Metadata is JsonObject and shallow merge. Dedicated top-level properties can be JsonArray/Object. We can transactionally update using `UserMetadataSQLStorage`. Yet code complexity and exceptions.

Could instead make repository in-memory concurrent hash maps keyed by main? That is not durable but enough for tests and no storage compatibility. But claim production feature weak. User asked add to SuperTokens; should use existing storage.

Could add new tables to embedded `inmemorydb` and perhaps define core-local store accessed by `StorageLayer.getStorage(main)` and pattern match `io.supertokens.inmemorydb.Start`. But external production plugins won't support. Hidden tests likely run inmemory only. Yet code would compile and be testable. More conventional for this repo? All recipe storage methods are in plugin interface and implemented in Start; modifying plugin interface outside repo isn't captured. But perhaps build resolves plugin interface from `../supertokens-plugin-interface` at a specific branch and we could modify that too? Final patch only current repo likely. We should not depend on outside edits.

Could use existing `UserMetadata` public static methods marked `@TestOnly`? The overload `updateUserMetadata(Main,...)` is @TestOnly but production API uses AppIdentifier/storage overload. We'll use production overload. It supports all SQL plugins. Good.

Need ensure metadata update transaction and concurrency. We can implement our own transaction with `StorageUtils.getUserMetadataStorage(storage)`:
```
UserMetadataSQLStorage md = StorageUtils.getUserMetadataStorage(storage);
return md.startTransaction(con -> {
 JsonObject root = md.getUserMetadata_Transaction(appId, con, userId);
 JsonObject state = root?.getAsJsonObject(KEY);
 ...
 root.add(KEY, state);
